WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:08.098
I work in cancer
research where I

00:00:08.098 --> 00:00:09.640
lead a computational
team that solves

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.360
challenges in drug development.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.690
But that's not what
I'm here to talk about.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.300
Like many of you, I'm really
excited to be here learning

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.170
about MLOps and tidy models
and quarto and all those things.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.240
And I think those are
all great skills that

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.570
help you be good at your job.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.450
However, I want to talk
to you about a skill

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.300
that I think can help
you be great at your job,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.752
and that is creativity.

00:00:29.752 --> 00:00:31.210
And I want to walk
you through some

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.168
of the steps that I took
in my creative process

00:00:33.168 --> 00:00:35.690
for using R to help
design a quilt.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.690
And hopefully that will
extend a little bit more

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.360
into how you can apply these
things to get better enjoyment

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.820
and fulfillment out of
your personal projects,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.320
but also your day job.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.170
And so first, I have to start
with a disclosure slide,

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.490
and my disclosure is that I'm
actually not a creative person

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.740
or I never thought
I was creative

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.560
because creative
people look like this.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.810
They have paint coming
out of their chest.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.530
They have light bulbs
around their head

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.819
and they're genetically
more creative.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:07.000
They have creative
parents and they just

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.690
know how to solve problems and
ideas come to them from heaven.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.560
And I went to engineering
school for almost 10 years,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.570
and I'm good at
math, and I like it

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.260
when there's a correct answer.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.270
And I can follow steps to
get to that correct answer.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.270
However, I'm in an industry that
requires a lot of innovation,

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.730
and I looked around at the
people who are doing inspiring

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:31.300
work around me and whether those
are people that report to me

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.920
or are people who are
influential in the field.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.450
They were very creative.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.080
And so I, as a
good professional,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.190
I've been on a journey over the
last some years to learn skills

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.230
and to practice
being more creative

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.410
and learning techniques to
also help teams and cultures be

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.230
more creative.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:53.130
So now quilting. For those of you
who don't know what a quilt is,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.040
it's a blanket.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.620
And quilting has
been in practice

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.430
for thousands of years.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.790
And what makes a quilt is
that you have three layers,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.460
you have a top, you have
an insulating middle layer

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.020
and you have a back layer.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.440
And to quilt, you connect
these layers together,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.970
usually with thread, and
this can be very decorative.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.370
It's an art form.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.710
And personally I
got really excited

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.700
about making quilts as
art, and particularly

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.530
the modern quilting movement.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.470
Oh, before I say that, I just
want to put this in R terms

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:23.950
that everyone can understand.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.170
Quilting, it's just fabric

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:27.960
you cut up, you sew
together, and then

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.420
you sew it together again.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.360
So back to modern quilting.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.360
This is an example of a
beautiful, modern quilt.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.170
It has these organic
shapes made out of fabric.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.648
This one I also love.
Extremely different,

00:02:42.648 --> 00:02:44.190
but it has this
beautiful composition

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.010
that draws your eye
around the frame.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.000
Quilts can also play with
color, like this example, which

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.750
is really challenging
to do given that you

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.000
don't have every color in
the rainbow available to you

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.560
in fabric.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.950
Quilts can also have
political messages or quilts

00:02:58.950 --> 00:02:59.830
can be personal.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:02.670
Like this is a self-portrait
someone made in fabric.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.250
So I was really
excited about this.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.150
And that brings me
to the prerequisite.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:10.200
The prerequisite is that you
are excited about your work.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.350
I don't think you need to be
the most experienced person.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.230
So I started quilting at
the beginning of 2021.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.033
I bought a sewing machine
a little over a year ago,

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:21.450
but I'm not going
to let that stop

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.310
me doing something interesting
if I'm excited about it.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.740
So the first step in
my creative process

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.520
is to come up with
too many ideas.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.960
I'm somebody that I like
to come up with one idea

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.040
and then execute
the crap out of it.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.710
Like, don't keep thinking
about other things.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.567
But my new method is that I
try to come up with more ideas

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:41.400
than I think I need.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.900
I have an idea quota
where I come up

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.860
with 10 ideas, 40 ideas,
50 ideas, and then

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.600
from that you end
up with new ideas

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.180
that you had never
originally thought on day one

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:52.190
and also takes you in
directions that lets

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.700
you better curate your ideas.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.680
And what I ended up
doing with this project

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.430
was coming up with a solution
where I could fuse two domains,

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.710
and I had just taken
a drawing class,

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.980
and I learned about the
concept of two point perspective.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.190
Two point perspective is a
way to make a 3D visualization

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:13.640
in two dimensions like
these cuboid shapes.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.950
And I also know about
R, and I thought, well,

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.200
I'm really excited about the
things that are happening

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.190
with generative art using R.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.010
Could I create some sort
of landscape in this way?

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.340
A 2D projection
of a 3D landscape

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.520
using R using
something like geom_polygon()

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.010
And so with that, I was
really happy with that idea,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:34.660
and I started to go forward.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.220
So step two was I did
a lot of improvisation.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.910
So I can start to learn, how
could I make this visualization

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:43.390
in R? How could I mess
around with the dimensions, how

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:47.740
many shapes I wanted, the size
of these, the overall aspect

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.820
ratio, the colors.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.340
And then I had to
do a lot of research

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.570
because the fact is that
the design that I had chosen

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.140
is not easily convertible to
fabric, which is not something

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:02.670
that I had planned for.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.800
So I had to come up with
some techniques that

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.570
would allow me to construct
my design in fabric.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.750
And that is where I want
to talk to you a little bit

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.030
about a technique I really
like, which is called

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.522
foundation paper piecing.

00:05:13.522 --> 00:05:14.980
It's a little bit
of a rabbit hole

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.050
I'll go down with you.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.680
So this is a technique
where you create a design

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.870
and you print it on
a piece of paper.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.960
So you have a design like this
and you actually sequentially

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.770
sew fabric to that design.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:28.630
So this is what the
back would look like.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.510
And then the front
looks like this.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.610
And this is an amazing
technique because it

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:36.360
allows you to do very, very
precise piecing of fabrics

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.100
together.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.300
Those lines are all
perfectly parallel.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.470
You can have very,
very small pieces.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.640
And this lends itself very much
to the technique and the design

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.320
that I wanted to make.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.620
But there's a problem in
that this technique also

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.230
imparts a new geometrical
constraint and the type

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.060
of the design you can create.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:57.060
So you can only add one
piece of fabric at a time

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.330
and you can only add one seam
at a time, which seems simple,

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.180
but it does impose a constraint
on the amount of design

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.570
that you can do.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.600
So for something like my
design, I'm in trouble

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.730
because this is not pieceable.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.250
This design, with this y
junction in the middle,

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.950
I cannot do because I would
have to add two pieces of fabric

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:17.060
at the same time.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:18.350
It doesn't work.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.200
So the way that people who
design these patterns work

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.480
is it's basically
like a Sudoku puzzle.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.460
It's all done by hand
where you look at this

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:28.730
and I say, well, I can't
do this in one block,

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:31.160
so I'll split it
into two, and then

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:33.810
I have to design where
my seams will go.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.080
And then I also have to
determine the correct order,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.960
because if I don't add these
pieces in the correct order,

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.570
it won't work.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:42.400
And I really thought
a lot about, okay, well,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.850
is there a way I could use
R or some other framework

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:47.110
to help me do this?

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:48.580
And I found a
wonderful paper that

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.280
was published just last year
by Mackenzie Leake and others

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.590
at Stanford University,
which actually

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:56.480
gets into the mathematical
foundation for doing this.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.870
And I really recommend,
if you're interested,

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.820
to go ahead and read this paper.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.730
They've also got some YouTube
videos and even a web app

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:06.550
that you can use to help
design your own designs.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:09.430
And so I don't really want
to talk about graph theory,

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.420
although I do a little bit,
which is that these designs are

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:16.210
then represented as hyper
graphs, where each piece is

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:18.880
a node in the graph and
each seam is an edge.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.010
And this allows you
then to determine

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.770
if your design is
pieceable and also

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:26.780
automatically derive the order
that you would add the pieces.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.510
It's really cool.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.880
So if we take a complicated
design like the one

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.520
I made of the R
logo on the left,

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.180
that's actually four pieces.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.280
And I can represent
them as four graphs.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.670
And from that I can determine
that my design was good

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:41.890
because it was pieceable
and also the order

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:43.400
that I need to add the pieces.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:44.680
So that was great.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:49.270
I'm good, except for now
I have to make the quilt.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.905
And execution is a really
hard part of creativity

00:07:51.905 --> 00:07:53.530
because if you just
have a lot of ideas

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:56.020
and you don't actually follow
through and finish them,

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:57.880
that's a problem.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.340
So I started to work
on my sewing machine

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:01.893
and I worked on it for a while.

00:08:01.893 --> 00:08:03.310
And one of the
things that I think

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:06.400
is really helpful as part
of a creative process

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.830
is to seek advice
or collaboration.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:11.680
So here I actually went
on quilting reddit,

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:14.050
of course that exists,
and I posted my quilt

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.370
and asked "somebody
give me some ideas here.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.240
I'm kind of stuck."

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.620
And people were really generous.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.745
They gave me these
schematics back like, hey,

00:08:20.745 --> 00:08:23.680
try this, try that,
which was fantastic.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.020
Another thing that
is part of executing

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.150
is that you're going
to make mistakes.

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:31.960
And the interesting thing
about a visual art like quilting

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.900
is that your
mistakes are visceral

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:36.760
and they're hard to hide.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.789
So in my git repo, I can
kind of hide my mistakes,

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:40.807
although I don't.

00:08:40.807 --> 00:08:42.640
I usually write like,
"big mistake," "big mess,"

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:43.900
"clean this up."

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.790
But when I have
something physical,

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:47.120
that mistake is there.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:48.640
And I think it
helps you get better

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.890
at making mistakes
because you can see them

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.110
and you can be like, it's OK.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:53.527
It's part of growing
and learning.

00:08:55.863 --> 00:08:57.280
And so then this
is the other part

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.340
that's really hard is that
you have to share your work.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:03.032
So I did bring
this quilt with me.

00:09:03.032 --> 00:09:04.365
And I have to put this mic down.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.360
So I think.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:23.460
Here we go. [applause]

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:25.380
Thank you for humoring
me, but I think

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.110
this is one of the first
ggplots ever made in fabric.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.640
Probably not the first,
but one of the first.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:36.450
But there is another step,
which is that oftentimes you

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.150
find that the question you
were asking when you started

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.670
is not actually the
most important question

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.190
or the right question that
you should have been asking.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:46.285
And that definitely
happened here.

00:09:46.285 --> 00:09:47.910
I started off and I
thought like, yeah,

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.220
I'll just make a plot and
then I'll make it in fabric.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:52.950
That's the right question.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:54.602
And I came up with
these other ideas,

00:09:54.602 --> 00:09:57.060
which are really interesting
research that I could actually

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.970
be performing as
part of this process.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:02.423
Everything from like, how do
I choose the right colors?

00:10:02.423 --> 00:10:03.840
How do I determine
how much fabric

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.100
to order to how do I try
to design these blocks more

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.210
intelligently?

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.160
So that's one of the
future work that I'm

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.840
working on at the moment.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:16.660
For example, the R logo is
really hard to make as a quilt.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.300
It has some curve shapes,
it has some square shapes.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.440
Really difficult to represent
that actually in fabric.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.970
So I could do something
sort of naive here.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.450
I can create a
pixelated design, which

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.400
I think it's
actually pretty cool,

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.860
but it doesn't really
look like the R logo

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.460
to me, so I wasn't
happy with that.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:37.940
So then I can do this, which is
just I kind of, mostly by hand,

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.218
generated this pattern.

00:10:39.218 --> 00:10:40.260
And I think that's great.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.060
I think it looks
just like the R logo.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.750
So success story,
except if I wanted

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:49.580
to go a step further and have
the code generate this for me,

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:50.790
that's what I'm working on.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:52.790
And if you're a
close observer, you

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:54.535
can see that this
doesn't look right.

00:10:57.045 --> 00:10:58.170
You don't have to humor me.

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.330
It's not right.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:02.320
But from this I am
learning a lot, right?

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.888
There's a process here and I'm
learning interesting things

00:11:04.888 --> 00:11:06.930
and I think it's leading
in different directions.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:08.520
I can take that kind of thing,

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.710
and I can make a self
portrait of myself.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:14.570
I'm working on other approaches
where I can have code generate

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:15.930
a design, but as well

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.820
the fabric choices
that you could use.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:21.400
So now that I've
described the process,

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.210
I have to be honest with you,
I didn't describe it entirely

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:27.943
in that, I kind of made up the order.
For the purpose of the talk,

00:11:27.943 --> 00:11:30.610
I thought it would be helpful to
say like one, two, three, four,

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:31.930
five, six.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:34.790
But in reality, that's not the
order that I did these things.

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:36.640
And that's true with a
lot of creative work,

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:40.000
whether you're doing it for your
day job or for your art projects,

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.520
generally, it's a very
non-linear process,

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.020
which is something
that I personally

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:45.437
struggle with
because I would like

00:11:45.437 --> 00:11:47.980
it to be a linear process
with like a right answer,

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:51.800
but that's something that can
help you strengthen your work.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:58.050
OK so I want to now talk about
a couple tortured metaphors

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:00.390
that I've learned
from other people

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:03.630
that I think apply to my work
in computational research

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:06.627
and hopefully also will
resonate with some of you

00:12:06.627 --> 00:12:08.460
and the kind of work
that you do and how you

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:10.555
could try to be more creative.

00:12:10.555 --> 00:12:12.930
So this first example, I
learned from a colleague of mine

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.900
at BMS, Joe Szustakowski,
and the question is,

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.510
would you rather be a pizza
deliverer or would you

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.730
rather be a plumber?

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:22.860
I'll let people think.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:24.670
What does it--how
do you get pizza?

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:26.970
I order pizza a
lot and it's great.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:29.850
I can get on my phone, I can
say what toppings I want,

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.300
how many pizzas, and
then I get a photo

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:35.290
of the pizza on my doorstep.

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:36.460
It's incredible, right?

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:38.970
It works really, really
well for food delivery.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:42.360
But I've also been on teams
where I'm a data analyst

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:46.080
and I'm asked for things in
the same way. Somebody goes on

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:47.680
and they order a data pizza.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:50.040
They say, I need this
dashboard with these features,

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.480
with this data.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:54.468
And I don't think that's a very
effective way to do data work.

00:12:54.468 --> 00:12:56.760
I don't think that's a good
way for you to get insights

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:58.590
from data.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.997
And it doesn't leave any
room for creativity or trying

00:13:00.997 --> 00:13:03.330
to get to what are really the
right questions you should

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.210
be asking.

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:07.420
So a slight iteration on that
is to think about a plumber.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:10.050
So a plumber is a subject
matter expert, right?

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:11.790
I have a plumber
come to my house

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.160
and they talk to me about
what my problems are.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:17.400
They bring their own tool kit,
they have their own expertise,

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.900
and they try to understand
really what my needs are.

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:23.520
And they work on a solution
that will meet my needs.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:26.410
OK So if you don't like
that one, that's fine.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:28.350
I have another one.

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:29.320
So this one is

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:33.200
Would you rather be a
detective or a criminal?

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:34.800
and it's a trick question,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.010
you actually want to be both.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.500
So this is actually one
from Albert Einstein.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:44.060
And so this is about explaining
why science is so difficult.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.550
So if you take a detective
like a Sherlock Holmes,

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:49.910
a murder's been
committed and you collect clues

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:52.610
and you have hypotheses like
it's a jealous girlfriend,

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:55.230
it's an ex-colleague
or whatever.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:56.780
And you put together
all these clues

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:58.572
and then you discover
like, oh, actually it

00:13:58.572 --> 00:14:00.620
was a poisonous jellyfish
that killed the person.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:03.140
Like, that's how a
detective works, right?

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:05.060
But the key is that
a detective knows

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:06.420
what crime they're solving.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.580
The problem is
really well defined.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:09.810
This person's been murdered.

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.120
This has been stolen.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:12.820
I know how to solve it.

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:14.770
But the difference
is for a scientist,

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:17.830
you have to also come
up with the questions.

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.290
So in that way, you
have to commit the crime

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:23.080
and you have to perform
the investigation.

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:25.600
And I find this is a really
apt metaphor for the work

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:28.720
that I do, because you have
to constantly be asking,

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.710
am I asking the right questions?

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:32.560
Is this the important
line of research

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:34.020
that I should be following?

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.430
And I think that's a really,
really important part

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:41.890
of doing science that
requires you to be creative.

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:46.750
So to wrap those thoughts up, I
would say you should be a plumber.

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:50.045
You should also
commit science crimes.

00:14:50.045 --> 00:14:51.670
And I would also
encourage you to think

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.950
about celebrating
your mistakes and also

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:55.690
getting exposure to new ideas.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:57.070
So I think at
meetings like this,

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.200
it's great to go to
talks that you might not

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:01.210
think would be interesting.

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:03.130
I would also encourage
you to find your local

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:06.400
R user group, R
Ladies group or meetup group,

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.950
to try to attend talks and
meet people that you might not

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:12.640
normally meet in your day job.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:15.610
With that, I have some
acknowledgments or resources

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:18.580
if you're interested in
learning more about creativity.

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:21.070
And this is also a work
in progress that I have,

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:24.910
and you might recognize
that that is geom_point().

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:25.460
All right.

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:27.210
Thank you.

